+# Sourced inspiration note
+
+## Parallel cited
+
+Lewis Spence, *The Myths of Mexico & Peru* (London: George G. Harrap & Co., 1913), Chapter V, “The Maya,” subsection “The Creation-Story,” printed p. 209. The quoted lines are delivered in `source-excerpt.md`; the online text is [Project Gutenberg eBook 53080](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/53080), full text at <https://www.gutenberg.org/files/53080/53080-h/53080-h.htm>.
+
+The excerpt reproduces Spence's English prose and his label “Kiche story.” This note attributes the quoted passage only to Spence's account; it does not treat it as a K'iche-language edition or authenticate the underlying narrative. It does not identify an original individual author, represent all Maya or K'iche traditions, or establish oral continuity. The fictional verse's “chalk stair” asks a limited design question about a name versus a place; it explicitly rejects the source passage's world-making outcome. It imports no named beings, plot, ritual, or cultural rule.
+
+## Attribution question
+
+The English prose and its interpretation as Spence's account are attributed to Lewis Spence and the cited 1913 edition, not to an identified ancient or contemporary K'iche author. The primary K'iche-language source is not reproduced or independently assessed here.
+
+## Rights question
+
+The rights status of this edition, its presentation, and the delivered excerpt has not been determined for all jurisdictions. Consult applicable terms before reuse. Neither age of the narrative nor public accessibility answers the rights question for this edition.
+
+## AI-training-permission question
+
+AI-training permission has not been assessed or established. Public accessibility, attribution, or a possible copyright conclusion does not imply permission for training; this question remains separate and unanswered.
+
+This note records sourced inspiration only. It is not an in-world source and supplies no fictional canon.
