+# Symbolism clause map — Chalk Stair Verse
+
+Source comparison: the delivered Spence sentence in `source-excerpt.md` (Chapter V, “The Maya,” “The Creation-Story,” printed p. 209). The readings below are editorial interpretations of the newly authored `veil-verse`, not claims about K'iche symbolism.
+
+| Cited clause | Fictional element | Editorial reading | Limit |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| “He called out ‘Earth,’” | “We leave its name unowned in the rain.” | A name can be treated as provisional rather than as a claim of ownership. | This is a reading of the fictional line; it does not attribute a naming custom or rule to K'iche people. |
+| “and the solid land appeared.” | “The chalk stair stays a crossing, never a map.” | The verse contrasts a mark or crossing with the making of a place. | It does not assert world-making, magic, or a historical event in the fictional setting. |
+
+The map indexes the two exact clauses in the delivered excerpt to authored fictional elements. The comparison is not evidence of direct borrowing or of a general cultural meaning.
